-- title:
--   Admissible values attained by y at places over a supersingular node
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a positive level $N$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring map $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$, modular polynomial data for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha, h\beta$ for the two Hecke maps $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-level $N \to Nq$, a place specialisation $P$ of these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$; assume $q \nmid N$, that $R$ satisfies the four divisor and cusp laws constituting `IsModel`, and that it satisfies `OrderLawFixed`. Let $K \subset \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be finite over $\mathbb{Q}$ and let $w$ be a place of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ N$ lying in $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\ q\ N\ k$, i.e. $w$ is rational, both geometric $j$-generators lie in its valuation ring, and its value at $jGeomGen$ lies in the supersingular $j$-set. Assume the value-integrality law at $w$: every element of $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\ w$ has its value in $A$ at every place $V$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(Nq)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\ V = w$. Assume further that every $g$ in the node ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$ (elements of $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\ w$ whose Laurent expansion lies in $\mathrm{fieldOver}(Nq)\ K$) differs from the constant $R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\ K\ w\ o$ attached to some $o$ in the coefficient ring $A \cap K$ by a non-unit, and that this node ring is local and Noetherian. Let $\varpi \in A \cap K$ generate the kernel of $\mathrm{redRestrict}\ \mathrm{red}\ K$ in the divisibility sense: $d$ reduces to $0$ if and only if $d = \varpi d'$ for some $d'$. Let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation domain with irreducible element $\pi$, let $\sigma$ be a ring map from $W$ to the adic completion of the node ring sending $\pi$ to the image of $R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\ K\ w\ \varpi$, let $E \geq 1$, and let $\iota$ be a ring isomorphism from that completion onto the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ (\pi^{E}) = W[[U,V]]/(UV - \pi^{E})$ carrying $\sigma(o)$ to the constant $o$ for each $o \in W$, and reading off branch orders: if the first residue of $f$ at $w$ is non-zero of order $n$ then $\iota(f) \equiv \gamma V^{n}$ modulo the ideal generated by the constant $\pi$ and $U$ for some unit $\gamma$, and if the second residue of $f$ at $\mathrm{arithFrobC}\ q\ k\ N \cdot w$ is non-zero of order $n$ then $\iota(f) \equiv \gamma U^{n}$ modulo the ideal generated by the constant $\pi$ and $V$ for some unit $\gamma$. Finally let $c = (x,y)$ be node coordinates of $R$ over $K$ at $w$ (so the first residue of $x$ vanishes, the second residue of $x$ has order $1$ at $\mathrm{arithFrobC}\ q\ k\ N \cdot w$, the second residue of $y$ vanishes, and the first residue of $y$ has order $1$ at $w$), and suppose $x y = (R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\ K\ w\ \varpi)^{E_{0}} u$ with $u$ a unit of the node ring. Then for every $c_{0} \in A$ lying in the maximal ideal of $A$, non-zero in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and such that $\varpi^{E_{0}} = c_{0} m$ for some $m$ in the maximal ideal of $A$, there is a place $V'$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(Nq)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\ V' = w$ and $V'.\mathrm{evalAt}(y) = c_{0}$.
--
--   This is the existence half of the defining property of the node annulus: on the model of $X_0(Nq)$ whose completed local ring at a supersingular point of the fibre at $q$ is the crossing model $W[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi^{E})$, the coordinate $y$ of a node datum attains every admissible value $c_{0}$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ that divides $\varpi^{E_{0}}$ with non-unit cofactor, at a place of the level-$Nq$ function field reducing to $w$. It is used by the uniqueness statement `existsUnique_reduceFst_eq_and_hasValue_y_of_orderLawFixed`, by the construction of the annulus parametrisation `exists_annulus_dom_iff_reduceFst_eq_and_param_eq_y_of_ringEquiv_uvCrossingModel`, and by the attachment of component charts to the annuli at the node.
